// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 2 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P7): one LOCAL text pushed into TWO qualifying
// LOCAL lists. Originally (4e): both `p7L1` and `p7L2` kept their
// scope-exit element-free loops (only the pushed VALUE was disqualified),
// so both loops freed the one handle `p7X` shares with them: SIGSEGV.
// Critical 2's rule disqualifies BOTH receivers' DEEP release,
// independently, the moment each push sees a non-fresh (bare-identifier)
// value -- still true today, unchanged by ARC Task 4 (container elements
// are Task 5's still-unmigrated slot class: push never retains, so a deep
// element-walk on either list would still double-free the shared handle).
//
// ARC Task 4 (counted named-slot stores + unconditional local release):
// `p7X` is disqualified for a DIFFERENT, still-genuinely-unsafe reason
// (`lowFreeUnsafe`): handed to TWO push arguments, neither a consumer this
// task's migration covers -- so `p7X` is SKIPPED entirely at scope exit,
// not shallow-released (see p6_dup_push_local.cla's own comment for the
// same reasoning, one push site instead of two). `p7L1`/`p7L2` themselves,
// by contrast, ARE real named-slot locals now covered by unconditional
// release -- each gets a genuine shallow release at scope exit (confirmed
// against the emitted C: bare `rt_list_release` for both, no element
// loop). Expected strict-mode leak count: 2 (see ../p7_two_lists.leaks,
// dropped from 6) -- `p7X`'s text only (struct box + data handle, 2
// blocks; the one handle both lists share, still never freed). Not a bug
// -- container-element ownership is explicitly out of this task's scope,
// staged for Task 5.

func runP7TwoLists() {
    var p7L1: list of text
    var p7L2: list of text
    var p7X: text = "shared"
    p7L1.push(p7X)
    p7L2.push(p7X)
    alert("p7 done")
}
